WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:05.439 --> 00:00:06.911
- [Narrator] There's a small
neighborhood in Montréal

00:00:06.911 --> 00:00:08.503
known as Mile End, which has become

00:00:08.503 --> 00:00:11.148
an unlikely hub for gaming companies.

00:00:11.148 --> 00:00:13.469
- It is now a neighborhood that has

00:00:13.469 --> 00:00:15.355
a high concentration of people who

00:00:15.355 --> 00:00:17.189
work in game development.

00:00:17.189 --> 00:00:18.410
The Mile End is the home of

00:00:18.410 --> 00:00:20.528
some of the most creative
and most recognized

00:00:20.528 --> 00:00:24.685
game ideas in the history
of modern video games.

00:00:24.685 --> 00:00:26.233
- [Narrator] One of
these is Minority Media,

00:00:26.233 --> 00:00:27.537
a company that's the catalyst for

00:00:27.537 --> 00:00:31.043
a burgeoning video game
genre called empathy gaming

00:00:31.043 --> 00:00:32.671
where players are forced to confront

00:00:32.671 --> 00:00:36.542
real human issues like bullying,
alcoholism, or depression.

00:00:36.542 --> 00:00:39.029
Gamers place themselves in
the shoes of the character

00:00:39.029 --> 00:00:42.493
to feel and try and understand
what they are going through.

00:00:42.493 --> 00:00:44.894
- What we're trying to do here is

00:00:44.894 --> 00:00:48.563
we're trying to put people
in a different mindset.

00:00:48.563 --> 00:00:52.321
We're trying to put people
into a different perspective.

00:00:52.321 --> 00:00:56.188
As Canadians, we're used to being able to

00:00:56.188 --> 00:00:59.732
put ourselves in other peoples' shoes.

00:00:59.732 --> 00:01:02.380
We're used to looking at things

00:01:02.380 --> 00:01:04.906
from a different perspective.

00:01:04.906 --> 00:01:07.875
Gaming can be for anybody, for everybody.

00:01:07.875 --> 00:01:11.585
We're taking advantage of the
educational possibilities.

00:01:11.585 --> 00:01:13.584
We've developed a language game

00:01:13.584 --> 00:01:16.972
for a school board up in northern Québec.

00:01:16.972 --> 00:01:18.973
They've approached us to do a game

00:01:18.973 --> 00:01:21.011
for the grade one students.

00:01:21.011 --> 00:01:24.268
We've worked with the curriculum
that they've established.

00:01:24.268 --> 00:01:26.423
We've developed the story behind that

00:01:26.423 --> 00:01:28.590
and made that into a game.

00:01:29.765 --> 00:01:31.231
- Empathy games themselves have

00:01:31.231 --> 00:01:33.026
become a less exclusive genre

00:01:33.026 --> 00:01:36.615
and have integrated
themselves into bigger games.

00:01:36.615 --> 00:01:38.489
And so, empathy games have influenced

00:01:38.489 --> 00:01:40.861
the way bigger games are made now.

00:01:40.861 --> 00:01:44.279
- There is room to go a lot deeper

00:01:44.279 --> 00:01:47.374
into what gaming can actually be.

00:01:47.374 --> 00:01:50.677
There's a universe of possibilities.

00:01:50.677 --> 00:01:52.306
And so, I think, uh, we've only

00:01:52.306 --> 00:01:55.139
just begun scratching the surface.

